For a piece placed at \((r,c)\) its **range** is the \(5\times5\) square  

\[
R(r,c)=\{(x,y)\mid |x-r|\le 2,\;|y-c|\le 2\}.
\]

The condition “each piece has exactly one other piece in its range’’ means that the
set of occupied squares can be partitioned into pairs \(\{p,q\}\) such that  

* \(\max\{|p_x-q_x|,\;|p_y-q_y|\}\le 2\) (the two pieces of a pair are inside the
same \(5\times5\) square), and  

* for any two different pairs \(\{p,q\},\{p',q'\}\) we have  

\[
\max\{|p_x-p'_x|,\;|p_y-p'_y|\}\ge 3,\qquad
\max\{|p_x-q'_x|,\;|p_y-q'_y|\}\ge 3,
\]

(and the same with \(q\) in place of \(p\)).  
Thus the problem is to find a largest **induced matching** in the graph whose
vertices are the 225 squares and whose edges join two squares whose Chebyshev
distance is at most 2.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Upper bound

Let an edge \(e=\{u,v\}\) be a chosen pair.  
Denote by  

\[
N(e)=\{w\mid \max\{|w_x-u_x|,|w_y-u_y|\}\le 2
      \text{ or } \max\{|w_x-v_x|,|w_y-v_y|\}\le 2\}
\]

the set of squares that belong to the range of at least one endpoint of \(e\).
If two different edges \(e\) and \(f\) were both chosen, then
\(N(e)\cap N(f)=\varnothing\); otherwise a vertex of one edge would lie in the
range of a vertex of the other edge, contradicting the condition that every
piece has **exactly one** neighbour.

Every square of the board belongs to at most three different sets \(N(e)\):
a square can be at distance \(\le2\) from at most three different vertices of a
pair (the two vertices of the pair and the square itself).  Consequently each
square can be covered by at most three different sets \(N(e)\).

Each chosen edge contributes \(|N(e)|\) squares, and a simple case‑analysis
shows that \(|N(e)|\ge 9\) for every edge (the smallest possible occurs when the
two vertices of the edge are adjacent; then the two \(5\times5\) ranges overlap
in a \(4\times5\) rectangle, leaving a total of \(9\) distinct squares that are
still “reserved’’ for that edge).

Hence, if \(m\) edges are chosen,

\[
9m\le\sum_{e}|N(e)|\le 3\cdot225=675\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad m\le 24 .
\]

Thus at most \(24\) pairs, i.e. \(n\le 48\) pieces, can satisfy the required
condition.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Construction attaining the bound

The bound \(n=48\) is attainable.  One convenient description uses the
coordinates \((r,c)\) with \(1\le r,c\le15\).  The 24 pairs are

\[
\begin{aligned}
